~ start
Chancellor Vogler: Herr President, my officials have drawn a high-speed line from Strasbourg to Dresden. It is either a bridge between our peoples or the most expensive tunnel in Saxony.
- Offer to co-finance the line => cofinance
- Suggest a freight corridor first => freight
- Ask about Berlin's defence spending instead => defence_ask
~ cofinance
Chancellor Vogler: Co-financing, from Paris? Then we must decide who owns the sleepers, which is where every European railway has gone to die.
- Commit French money now => rail_commit
- Insist on a joint operating company => rail_company
~ rail_commit
Chancellor Vogler: Then the line will have a groundbreaking, a ribbon, and a French subsidy, in that order of importance.
=> END
~ rail_company
Chancellor Vogler: A joint company. Eighteen months of lawyers, and then a train. I have seen worse arrangements.
=> END
~ freight
Chancellor Vogler: Freight is honest work. Nobody cuts a ribbon for a wagon of steel, but the Ruhr will remember who made it cheaper.
- Back the freight corridor => freight_yes
- Keep it to a feasibility paper => freight_paper
~ freight_yes
Chancellor Vogler: Good. My transport minister will faint, and then she will send you flowers.
=> END
~ freight_paper
Chancellor Vogler: A paper. Our officials will each write half and disagree about the other half, as tradition requires.
=> END
~ defence_ask
Chancellor Vogler: Berlin spends what the Bundestag permits, which is never what Paris asks. But you may try, between the rivers.
- Press for a joint procurement pact => procure
- Drop it and enjoy the journey => drop_it
~ procure
Chancellor Vogler: A pact. Our generals would have to share a catalogue. I give it a year before someone claims it was their idea.
=> END
~ drop_it
Chancellor Vogler: Wise. A sleeper car is no place for a budget argument, and the schnapps is excellent.
=> END
